From: Ashwin <AshwinKhan@student.tudelft.nl>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 11:59:25 +0200
Subject: Reserve the circuit's actual size, not the bound

Before a circuit can be used it is decompressed into a buffer, and three
places size that buffer with kCircuitSizeMax (130 MB) rather than with the
file. The circuits in use decompress to 88-115 MB, so 15-42 MB is reserved
for nothing.

The cost is not merely address space. std::vector value-initialises, so
every byte of the bound is written, which forces the kernel to back all
130 MB with physical pages. The bound is paid in full on every call.

zstd records the decompressed size in the frame header. Reading it and
reserving exactly that keeps kCircuitSizeMax as what it should be, an
acceptance limit, and stops it being an allocation size as well. One
number was doing two jobs, and they pull in opposite directions: lowering
it to save memory refuses legitimate circuits, raising it to accept larger
ones wastes more.

Measured on a Dimensity 8100, v6 1-attribute circuit: peak RSS 211.8 MB
to 171.2 MB. Proving and verifying get slightly faster as a side effect,
since the pages no longer have to be faulted in.
